From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] spmi: mtk-pmif: clear the RCS events of SIDs without a handler

The RCS interrupt is a level interrupt, and only the EOI of a SID's chained
interrupt clears an event from the SPMI_SLV_x_y_EINT registers. So the
event of a SID without a handler stays: the chained handler skips a SID
that isn't enabled, and doesn't even read the registers above the one of
irq_max_sid. The parent interrupt fires again as soon as it is unmasked.
Nothing ends this, since the spurious interrupt detection doesn't see
chained handlers. That CPU spends nearly all its time here from then on.
The handler still serves the SIDs that have one, but whatever needs that
CPU has to wait.

Such an event can happen. On the Lenovo TB375FC only the MT6363 at SID 4
and the MT6368 at SID 5 have handlers. The MT6685 at SID 9 raises one for
its RTC alarm, and the MediaTek RTC driver arms that alarm at shutdown if
Android has a power-off alarm set, so it may go off under Linux. An event
can also come in before the driver of a PMIC has probed.

Read all four registers, and clear the event of a SID that isn't enabled.
The MediaTek driver treats such events the same way: rcs_irq_handler(), in
the OnePlus MT6897 kernel and in the stock module of this tablet, reads the
status of all 16 SIDs on every interrupt and clears any event it finds,
before it looks at whether the SID is enabled. The four registers are
there: this handler only runs on SPMI 2.0 buses, whose register table has
them, mtk_spmi_irq_init() already writes all four, and the stock module has
them at the same offsets.

SIDs with a handler go on as before. An event cleared while the driver of a
PMIC is yet to set up its chained handler is no loss: mtk-spmi-pmic masks
and clears all the interrupts of the PMIC and writes its RCS done register
in probe, before that, so the handler would find nothing to do.

What this doesn't do:

 - Touch the chip. The source of the event stays there, as nothing here
   knows what it is. A chip that raises the event again right after it is
   cleared, for as long as its source is there, still keeps this handler
   running. Whether the MT6685 does that can only be seen on a device: the
   RCS done register of the MT63xx PMICs says nothing about it, since the
   MediaTek drivers don't use that register on the MT6685. So log the
   cleared events, with a rate limit. They are the only trace of this.

 - Cover a chained handler that was removed. The irq core doesn't call
   .irq_disable for that, so the SID stays enabled, and an event that comes
   in when the driver of a PMIC has unbound stays as well.

 - Keep the events of a SID that is disabled with disable_irq(). They used
   to wait for enable_irq(), with the parent interrupt firing all along.
   Nothing does that to these interrupts today.

irq_min_sid and irq_max_sid have no users left. irq_min_sid never moved in
any case: it starts at zero and could only go down.

Fixes: ab1b3469fc28 ("spmi: mtk-pmif: Implement Request Capable Slave (RCS) interrupt")
Link: https://github.com/OnePlusOSS/android_kernel_modules_and_devicetree_oneplus_mt6897/blob/76792af3e1fefabd34be6dd684bfc9facc4d5b17/kernel/kernel_device_modules-6.1/drivers/spmi/spmi-mtk-pmif-core.c
---
 drivers/spmi/spmi-mtk-pmif.c | 36 ++++++++++++++++--------------------
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 20 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/spmi/spmi-mtk-pmif.c b/drivers/spmi/spmi-mtk-pmif.c
index c605643..397800d 100644
--- a/drivers/spmi/spmi-mtk-pmif.c
+++ b/drivers/spmi/spmi-mtk-pmif.c
@@ -63,8 +63,6 @@ struct pmif_bus {
 	int irq;
 	struct clk_bulk_data clks[PMIF_MAX_CLKS];
 	size_t nclks;
-	u8 irq_min_sid;
-	u8 irq_max_sid;
 	u16 irq_en;
 	raw_spinlock_t	lock;
 };
@@ -496,34 +494,38 @@ static void mtk_spmi_handle_chained_irq(struct irq_desc *desc)
 	struct pmif_bus *pbus = irq_desc_get_handler_data(desc);
 	struct irq_chip *chip = irq_desc_get_chip(desc);
 	struct pmif *arb = to_mtk_pmif(pbus->ctrl);
-	u8 regidx_min, regidx_max;
 	bool irq_handled = false;
 	unsigned int i;
 
-	regidx_min = pbus->irq_min_sid / 4;
-	regidx_min += SPMI_SLV_3_0_EINT;
-
-	regidx_max = pbus->irq_max_sid / 4;
-	regidx_max += SPMI_SLV_3_0_EINT;
-
 	chained_irq_enter(chip, desc);
 
-	for (i = regidx_min; i <= regidx_max; i++) {
+	for (i = SPMI_SLV_3_0_EINT; i <= SPMI_SLV_F_C_EINT; i++) {
 		u32 val = mtk_spmi_readl(arb, pbus, i);
 
 		while (val) {
 			u8 bit = __ffs(val);
 			u8 bank = bit / 8;
 			u8 sid = ((i - SPMI_SLV_3_0_EINT) * 4) + bank;
+			u8 sta = val >> (8 * bank);
+			u32 mask = PMIF_RCS_IRQ_MASK << (8 * bank);
 
-			val &= ~(PMIF_RCS_IRQ_MASK << (8 * bank));
+			val &= ~mask;
+			irq_handled = true;
 
-			/* Check if IRQs for this SID are enabled */
-			if (!(pbus->irq_en & BIT(sid)))
+			/*
+			 * Only the EOI of a SID's handler clears its event.
+			 * Left alone, it would keep the parent interrupt
+			 * asserted, and this function would keep running.
+			 */
+			if (!(pbus->irq_en & BIT(sid))) {
+				mtk_spmi_writel(arb, pbus, mask, i);
+				dev_info_ratelimited(&pbus->ctrl->dev,
+						     "cleared event 0x%02x of SID %u: not enabled\n",
+						     sta, sid);
 				continue;
+			}
 
 			generic_handle_domain_irq_safe(pbus->dom, sid);
-			irq_handled = true;
 		}
 	}
 
@@ -598,12 +600,6 @@ static int mtk_spmi_rcs_irq_translate(struct irq_domain *d, struct irq_fwspec *f
 	*out_hwirq = intspec[0];
 	*out_type = intspec[2] & IRQ_TYPE_SENSE_MASK;
 
-	if (pbus->irq_min_sid > intspec[0])
-		pbus->irq_min_sid = intspec[0];
-
-	if (pbus->irq_max_sid < intspec[0])
-		pbus->irq_max_sid = intspec[0];
-
 	dev_dbg(dev, "Found SPMI IRQ %u (map: 0x%lx)\n", intspec[0], *out_hwirq);
 
 	return 0;
